---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const pctCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'old', label: 'old value', min: 10, max: 1000, step: 5, value: 200 },
    { name: 'new', label: 'new value', min: 10, max: 1000, step: 5, value: 260 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'change (new − old)', expr: 'new - old' },
    { label: 'percentage change', expr: '(new - old)/old*100', unit: '%' },
    { label: 'new as ratio of old', expr: 'new/old' },
    { label: 'reverse: old × (1 + pct)', expr: 'old*(1 + (new - old)/old)' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/ratios-percentages-proportion">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A ratio is a comparison frozen into a number; a percentage is just a ratio out of 100; and
    <strong>proportion</strong> is the assumption that a ratio stays fixed as quantities change.
    Nearly every headline statistic, shop discount and recipe is one of these three — and most
    statistical lies are one of them misused.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="percentage change, computed honestly — drag both values and watch the baseline do the work" widget="sliderLab" config={pctCfg} />

  <p>
    The subtle point the widget exposes: percentage change always divides by the <em>old</em>
    value, so the answer depends on which direction you travel. A rise from 100 to 150 is +50%;
    the fall from 150 back to 100 is −33%. "Up 50%, down 50%" does not return to the start.
  </p>

  <h2>The three honest habits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Name the baseline.</strong> "50% faster" than what? A percentage without a stated base is a rumour.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct vs inverse proportion:</strong> more workers → faster job (inverse, t ∝ 1/n); more flour → more bread (direct, b ∝ f). Mixing them up is a classic exam trap.</li>
    <li><strong>Percentage points ≠ percent.</strong> Interest rising from 2% to 3% is one percentage point, but a 50% increase. News reports swap these constantly.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When you reach exponential growth in this ladder, you'll see the deep version of this lesson:
    constant <em>ratio</em> growth per step is a whole different animal from constant
    <em>amount</em> growth.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Prealgebra & Elementary Algebra; Paulos, Innumeracy.</p>
</TopicPage>
